<BaseLayout title="Principles | Economy for Everyone">
  <section class="prose-e4e">
    <div class="ledger-header">
      <h2 class="title-reset">Principles</h2>
    </div>

    <p>
      This is the non-negotiable layer behind Economy for Everyone. The model explains the mechanics.
      The principles define the lines we do not cross while trying to fix them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The short version: human dignity applies to everyone, government power needs guardrails, truth
      matters, simplicity matters, and systems that shape life outcomes should be understandable and
      challengeable in practice.
    </p>

    <div class="ledger-header">
      <h3 class="title-reset">Non-Negotiables</h3>
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li>Human dignity applies to everyone.</li>
      <li>Government power needs guardrails: due process, oversight, and transparency.</li>
      <li>Accountability matters more than brand protection.</li>
      <li>People should be able to understand and challenge consequential automated decisions.</li>
      <li>If exit is weak, the governance bar has to rise.</li>
      <li>Truth matters, but it is not a weapon.</li>
      <li>Compassion does not require surrendering boundaries.</li>
      <li>Simplicity is a constitutional constraint: clear rules, fast enforcement, low friction for normal people.</li>
      <li>Neutrality is a behavioral claim, not a label: if enforcement is asymmetric, the rule is not neutral.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="ledger-header">
      <h3 class="title-reset">What This Means</h3>
    </div>
    <p>
      In practical terms, this project is pro-competition, pro-delivery, anti-rigging, and wary of
      systems that only work for people with time, money, or expert help.
    </p>
    <p>
      The newer AI case-study work sharpened three things in particular:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>where exit is weak, governance has to substitute for competition</li>
      <li>rights are not real if notices are vague, records are unreachable, or appeals arrive too late</li>
      <li>accountability fails when visibility is one-sided or responsibility dissolves across vendor chains</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="ledger-header">
      <h3 class="title-reset">Minimum Floor</h3>
    </div>
    <p>
      If a system shapes a life outcome, the minimum floor is:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>notice</li>
      <li>reason</li>
      <li>appeal</li>
      <li>records</li>
      <li>human override</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      That is not an AI-only idea. It is a basic legitimacy test for any high-impact system people
      cannot realistically escape.
    </p>

    <div class="ledger-header">
      <h3 class="title-reset">Neutral Rule Test</h3>
    </div>
    <p>A rule is not neutral if enforcement is asymmetric.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Who gets investigated?</li>
      <li>Who gets protected?</li>
      <li>Who gets treated as disposable or turned into a crime scene?</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      If equal protection, due process, or enforcement symmetry breaks in practice, the rule is not
      neutral no matter how clean the language sounds.
    </p>

    <div class="ledger-header">
      <h3 class="title-reset">Read Next</h3>
    </div>
    <p>
      Read the <a href="/core-model">core model</a> for the loop and the mechanism layer, or go to
      <a href="/playbooks">playbooks</a> for practical action.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
